2
00:00:30.000 --> 00:01:07.000
presents Laura versus fruit flies. Swarming your fruit and terrorizing your kitchen, these little freaks multiply at a rate that would make a rabbit say, yo, chill. But Laura shopped on Amazon and saved on cleaning spray, countertop wipes, and fly traps. Hey, fruit flies, your baby boom ends here. Save the Everyday with Amazon. Welcome aboard Via Rail. Please sit and enjoy. Please sit and sip. Play.
为您呈现：劳拉大战果蝇。这些小怪物成群结队地出现在你的水果上，恐吓着你的厨房，它们的繁殖速度快到会让兔子都说：“嘿，冷静点。”但劳拉在亚马逊上购物，省下了清洁喷雾、台面湿巾和捕蝇器的钱。嘿，果蝇们，你们的婴儿潮到此为止了。用亚马逊节省日常开支。欢迎乘坐维亚铁路（Via Rail）。请坐下享受旅程。请坐下小酌一杯。播放。

3
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:34.000
Post. Taste. View and enjoy. Via Rail, love the way. They are punk rockers. The new craze, they tell me. They're heroes. Not the nice, clean rolling stones.
发布。品味。观看并享受。维亚铁路，爱上这段旅程。他们是朋克摇滚乐手。他们告诉我，这是新潮流。他们是英雄。不是那种温和、干净的滚石乐队。

4
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:56.000
You see, they're as drunk as I am. They're clean by comparison. They're a group called the sex pistols, and I am surrounded by all of them. Now, I want to know one thing. Beethoven, Mozart, Bach and Brabts. They're all heroes of ours, isn't they? Really? What? What were you saying, sir? Oh, they're wonderful people.
你看，他们和我一样醉。相比之下，他们还算干净。他们是一个叫“性手枪”（Sex Pistols）的乐队，我被他们所有人包围着。现在，我想知道一件事。贝多芬、莫扎特、巴赫和勃拉姆斯。他们都是我们的英雄，不是吗？真的吗？什么？您刚才说什么，先生？哦，他们是了不起的人。

5
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Are they? Oh, yes. They really turn us on. Well, suppose they turn other people on. That's just they're tough shit. It's what? Nothing. A rude word. Next question. No, no. What was the rude word? Shit. Was it really? Good heavens, you frightened me to death. So that was an archive recording of the appearance by the punk rockers, the sex pistols, on the early evening TV show today, which went out
是吗？哦，是的。他们确实让我们兴奋。好吧，假设他们让其他人也兴奋。那是他们倒霉。那是……什么？没什么。一个粗俗的词。下一个问题。不，不。那个粗俗的词是什么？“Shit”（狗屎）。真的是吗？天哪，你吓死我了。所以，那段档案录音是朋克摇滚乐队“性手枪”在傍晚电视节目《今天》中的亮相，该节目播出时……

6
00:02:30.000 --> 00:03:10.000
only in London, actually, not nationally, just before 7pm on the evening of the 1st of December 1976. And the presenter as voiced by Dominic was Bill Grundy. I mean, the poor man, he became completely defined by that one interview, didn't they? It's basically the only thing he's remembered for now. And he completely lost control of the interview. And for some inexplicable reason, he just kept kind of goading them into coming out with more and more foul language. I suppose the big question is, was he actually drunk as he said he was at the start of the show? It doesn't sound massively drunk, but maybe he was good at hiding it. Well, we discussed how I should do that beforehand, didn't we?
实际上只在伦敦播出，并没有在全国范围内播放，时间是1976年12月1日傍晚7点之前。由多米尼克配音的主持人是比尔·格伦迪（Bill Grundy）。我的意思是，这个可怜的人，他完全被那一次采访定义了，不是吗？这基本上是他现在唯一被人们记住的事情。他完全失去了对采访的控制。出于某种无法解释的原因，他只是不断地怂恿他们说出越来越多的脏话。我想最大的问题是，他当时真的像他在节目开始时说的那样喝醉了吗？听起来并不像是烂醉如泥，但也许他很擅长掩饰。好吧，我们之前讨论过我该怎么做，不是吗？

7
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:34.000
And you said I should do it slurring my words, obviously drunk. But actually, when you watch the clip, he isn't obviously drunk, I wouldn't say? I mean, it's just inexplicable why he goes them into more and more swearing, and we will come to this, weren't we? I mean, Sex Pistols thought he was drunk when they were asked about it later. They did, yeah. But he got through the rest of the program, okay, this is just at the very end of the program. And this is the moment when the Sex Pistols kind of... blowed on to national consciousness.
你说我应该含糊不清地说话，表现得明显喝醉了。但实际上，当你观看这段视频时，他并不明显是醉了，我不会这么说？我的意思是，他为什么怂恿他们说越来越多的脏话，这简直无法解释，我们稍后会谈到这一点，不是吗？我的意思是，后来当被问及此事时，“性手枪”乐队认为他喝醉了。他们确实这么认为。但他完成了节目的其余部分，没问题，这只是在节目的最后。这就是“性手枪”乐队……闯入全国公众意识的时刻。

8
00:03:34.000 --> 00:04:02.000
Yes, it is. So this is 1976, and the next day, they were on the front pages of all the newspapers, the great headline in the mirror, I think it is, the filth and the fury. And from that moment on, they became household names. It's a rocker-row spindle. So I would say that if you look back at the previous 20 years, only the Beatles and the Rolling Stones had matched that level of fame or notoriety. And here's the funny thing.
是的，确实如此。所以这是1976年，第二天，他们登上了所有报纸的头版，我想《镜报》上的大标题是“污秽与愤怒”（The Filth and the Fury）。从那一刻起，他们成了家喻户晓的名字。这真是一场摇滚风暴。所以我认为，如果你回顾过去20年，只有披头士乐队和滚石乐队达到了那种名声或臭名昭著的程度。有趣的事情来了。

9
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:30.000
At this point, the Sex Pistols had not released a single record. It's Malcolm McLaren's masterpiece, isn't it? Yeah, that they are a media phenomenon in a way before they're a musical phenomenon. Anyway, we will come on to this interview later in the show, and maybe Tom will play another bit of that excellent archive footage. Because Susie Sue, as in The Banshees, is also there. I think it's the first TV appearance. I'm looking forward to hearing her.
在这一点上，“性手枪”乐队还没有发行过一张唱片。这是马尔科姆·麦克拉伦（Malcolm McLaren）的杰作，不是吗？是的，他们在成为音乐现象之前，在某种程度上先成为了一种媒体现象。无论如何，我们稍后会在节目中谈到这次采访，也许汤姆会播放另一段精彩的档案片段。因为苏西·苏（Siouxsie Sioux），也就是“苏西与女妖”乐队的主唱，当时也在场。我想这是她的第一次电视亮相。我很期待听到她的声音。

10
00:04:30.000 --> 00:05:12.000
Yeah. So what's so interesting about this is that this was broadcast on the very evening that one of the most humiliating political and economic dramas in modern British history was coming to a climax. So it's an intersection point between two different but arguably related things. So the first is the economic crisis that we've been tracing through this series. The pound and free fall, Britain staring into the economic abyss and the new Prime Minister, Jim Callaghan. he has been forced to go to the International Monetary Fund for a humiliating record bailout of almost $4 billion. So his cabinet is divided, and this very evening is the moment of decision.
是的。所以这最有趣的地方在于，这正是在现代英国历史上最令人羞辱的政治和经济戏剧之一达到高潮的那个晚上播出的。所以这是两个不同但可以说相关的事情的交汇点。首先是我们在这系列节目中一直追踪的经济危机。英镑处于自由落体状态，英国凝视着经济深渊，新任首相吉姆·卡拉汉（Jim Callaghan）被迫向国际货币基金组织（IMF）寻求近40亿美元的创纪录羞辱性救助。所以他的内阁四分五裂，而这个晚上正是决定的时刻。

11
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:46.000
And the second thing is that against the backdrop of the economic crisis, the inflation, the surge in youth unemployment, the sense of national failure and national breakdown, this new kind of music has come out of Britain's art colleges and pubs and clubs. It was initially called by the press Dole Cue. rock, better known as punk, and embodied by John Leiden or Johnny Rotten and his fellow sex pistols. And this is the evening that defines them forever as the ultimate 1970s folk devils. And so this is the story of today's episode, the intersection of these two stories, obviously
第二件事是，在经济危机、通货膨胀、青年失业率飙升、国家失败感和国家崩溃感的背景下，这种新音乐从英国的艺术学院、酒吧和俱乐部中涌现出来。它最初被媒体称为“救济金摇滚”（Dole Cue rock），更广为人知的名字是朋克，由约翰·莱顿（John Lydon）或约翰尼·罗顿（Johnny Rotten）和他的“性手枪”队友所体现。这就是那个让他们永远被定义为20世纪70年代终极“民间恶魔”的夜晚。所以这就是今天这一集的故事，这两个故事的交汇，显然……

12
00:05:46.000 --> 00:06:18.000
the primary focus being on the political one, and it's the climax of our series on 1975 and 1976. And we'll start with the main character in the drama, and now we introduced last time, top British Prime Minister, Jim Callaghan. So last time, Callaghan won the Labour leadership in the last episode, but we didn't really dig into his personality. And now at last, after 700 episodes of the rest is history or whatever it is, we finally get to talk about one of the undisputed Great Britons, and that is Sonny Jim.
主要焦点是政治故事，这是我们关于1975年和1976年系列节目的高潮。我们将从戏剧中的主角开始，我们上次介绍过，英国顶级首相吉姆·卡拉汉。上次，卡拉汉在上一集中赢得了工党领袖的职位，但我们并没有真正深入挖掘他的个性。现在终于，在《历史的其余部分》（The Rest Is History）或其他什么节目的700集之后，我们终于可以谈谈无可争议的伟大英国人之一，那就是“桑尼·吉姆”（Sonny Jim）。

13
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:50.000
So he was born Leonard James Callaghan in Portsmouth in 1912, in a working class house in the shadow of the Docks, the Royal Navy Docks. His father was a chief petty officer who died when Callahan was nine. Callahan was called Len, but when he became an adult, he renamed himself James, because James was his father's name. So it's a little sort of nod to his late father, which I think is very nice. It wasn't that he thought that James would he'd be likely to become Prime Minister? I don't think so, because Len Murray at the same time became head of the TUC.
他于1912年出生在朴茨茅斯的伦纳德·詹姆斯·卡拉汉，住在一座位于皇家海军码头阴影下的工人阶级房子里。他的父亲是一名首席士官，在卡拉汉九岁时去世。卡拉汉当时叫伦（Len），但当他成年后，他把自己改名为詹姆斯，因为詹姆斯是他父亲的名字。所以我认为这对他已故父亲是一种很好的致敬。并不是说他认为叫詹姆斯就更有可能成为首相？我不这么认为，因为伦·默里（Len Murray）在同一时间成为了英国工会联盟（TUC）的负责人。

14
00:06:50.000 --> 00:07:05.000
Len is a union name. I think James is the Prime Minister name. I think he would still have been Prime Minister if he was called Len. So he is shaped by his father's background to some extent. So the big thing he gets from his father is his love of the Royal Navy. And we admire that, don't we, Tom? We respect that. Very much.
伦是一个工会名字。我认为詹姆斯是一个首相名字。我想如果他叫伦，他仍然会成为首相。所以他在某种程度上受到他父亲背景的影响。他从父亲那里得到的最重要的东西就是他对皇家海军的热爱。我们很钦佩这一点，不是吗，汤姆？我们非常尊重这一点。非常尊重。

15
00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:25.000
He adores the Navy. He adores its service, its duty, its patriotism, comradeship. Later on in life, he collects naval prints. Oh, like Keith Richards. Yes, exactly. He would have bonded with Keith Richards over Naval Prince. He sees the Navy, I think, as a model for the Labour Party and indeed for Britain. And then what he gets from his mother is his back.
他崇拜海军。他崇拜它的服务、它的职责、它的爱国主义、同志情谊。晚年，他收集海军版画。哦，就像基思·理查兹（Keith Richards）一样。是的，没错。他会因为海军版画而与基思·理查兹建立联系。我认为他将海军视为工党乃至英国的典范。然后他从母亲那里得到的是他的……

16
00:07:25.000 --> 00:08:04.000
Baptist faith. So his mother is a very strict Baptist. He went to Sunday school every week. He learned passages of the Bible and he was very comfortable with kind of biblical references. Actually, it's something he has in common with Margaret Thatcher. I was just thinking that. I was thinking how many of the leading politicians in this generation and period come from low church backgrounds and how the collapse of kind of low church backgrounds in politics, I think is a bit of a tragedy. I totally agree with you. It gives people a sort of moral core, doesn't it? And not just a moral core. It gives them a language, I think. Yeah, a sensibility. Exactly, all of that kind of thing, which Callaghan and Thatcher in their different ways, completely embody. And it gives him actually that, the Baptist
浸信会信仰。所以他的母亲是一位非常严格的浸信会教徒。他每周都去主日学校。他学习圣经段落，并且对圣经引用非常熟悉。实际上，这是他和玛格丽特·撒切尔（Margaret Thatcher）的共同点。我刚才就在想这个。我在想，这一代和这一时期的许多主要政治家都来自低教会背景，而低教会背景在政治中的崩溃，我认为是一个悲剧。我完全同意你的看法。这给了人们一种道德核心，不是吗？不仅仅是道德核心。我认为这给了他们一种语言。是的，一种感性。没错，所有这些东西，卡拉汉和撒切尔以各自不同的方式完全体现了这一点。这实际上给了他，浸信会……

17
00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:43.000
stuff and the Royal Navy gives him this profound cultural conservatism, which we'll talk about in a second. He left Portsmouth Northern secondary school when he was 17. He became a tax clerk, then trade union official. He served in the East Indies fleet in the Second World War. He became a very young Labour MP in 1945. And then he was a junior minister. at the age of 35, and through sheer sort of doggedness and resilience, he made it to the top. He was a useless chancellor in the 1960s under Harold Wilson. But then Wilson gave him another chance as Home Secretary. He was very sort of solid, I think.
以及皇家海军的经历赋予了他这种深刻的文化保守主义，我们稍后会谈到。他17岁时离开了朴茨茅斯北部中学。他成为了一名税务员，然后是工会官员。他在第二次世界大战期间在东印度舰队服役。他于1945年成为一名非常年轻的工党议员。然后他在35岁时担任初级部长。凭借纯粹的坚韧和毅力，他登上了顶峰。他在20世纪60年代哈罗德·威尔逊（Harold Wilson）手下担任财政大臣时表现平平。但后来威尔逊给了他另一个机会，让他担任内政大臣。我认为他非常稳重。

18
00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:59.000
He loved the police, so he sort of, home secretary suited him. Late 60s, is the period when the kind of permissive society is. Yeah, but he hates the permissive society, and the people love that. Yeah. He's very much into policemen cracking. students over the head. Or giving them a clip around the ear. I think that's what he would say.
他热爱警察，所以内政大臣这个职位很适合他。60年代末是那种“宽容社会”的时期。是的，但他讨厌宽容社会，而人们喜欢这一点。是的。他非常支持警察敲打学生的脑袋。或者给他们一个耳光。我想他会这么说。

19
00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:30.000
Give them a clip around the year and tell them to get their hair cut. And the Tom Holland tendency shake their head sadly at that, but the Dominic Sam Brooks cheer and applaud and tears of pride spring to their eyes. So I think that's very much his vibe. And then he was Foreign Secretary in the early 70s. In his politics, he is the quintessential embodiment of the old Labour right. So the right of the party, but sort of backwards. looking, old-fashioned, moderate, pragmatic, patriotic.
给他们一个耳光，告诉他们去理发。汤姆·霍兰德（Tom Holland）倾向的人会对此悲伤地摇头，但多米尼克·桑德布鲁克（Dominic Sandbrook）倾向的人会欢呼鼓掌，自豪的泪水涌上眼眶。所以我认为这非常符合他的风格。然后他在70年代初担任外交大臣。在政治上，他是老工党右翼的典型化身。所以是党的右翼，但有点守旧、老派、务实、务实、爱国。

20
00:09:30.000 --> 00:10:03.000
Patriotic is a key word, isn't it? The friend of the British working man, that's his thing. So he was the parliamentary spokesman for the Police Federation, and that really sums him up. He's a big guy, he's breezy, as we said, by law, you have to call him avuncular. He has this bruiser reputation, but he has flashes of self-doubt, because as we learned last time, The fact that he didn't go to university and that he's not an intellectual weighs quite heavily on him, his great rival was Roy Jenkins who absolutely despised him. Bailey, old man.
爱国是一个关键词，不是吗？英国工人的朋友，这就是他的风格。所以他是警察联合会的议会发言人，这真的总结了他的一切。他是个大块头，他很轻松，正如我们所说，法律规定你必须称他为“和蔼可亲的”。他有这种“打手”的名声，但他也有自我怀疑的时刻，因为正如我们上次了解到的，他没有上过大学且不是知识分子的事实对他影响很大，他最大的竞争对手是罗伊·詹金斯（Roy Jenkins），他绝对鄙视他。贝利，老兄。

21
00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:30.000
Roy Jenkins said later, there is no case I can think of in history where a man combines such a powerful political personality with so little intelligence. But only one man in British history, as we said last time, has been Chancellor, Home Secretary, Foreign Secretary and Prime Minister. And that man was not Roy Jenkins. it was Jim Callahan. However, Jim Callahan did not become European Commissioner. Yeah, he wouldn't have wanted to, I think. It's not really his vibe.
罗伊·詹金斯后来曾说，在历史上我能想到的任何案例中，没有一个人能将如此强大的政治个性和如此少的智慧结合在一起。但正如我们上次所说，英国历史上只有一个人担任过财政大臣、内政大臣、外交大臣和首相。那个人不是罗伊·詹金斯，而是吉姆·卡拉汉。然而，吉姆·卡拉汉没有成为欧盟委员。是的，我想他也不会想成为。那真的不是他的风格。

22
00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:51.000
Yeah, I'm just saying, I'm just sticking out for Woy. Sometimes people say of Callahan, I think not unreasonably, he was the last true conservative to be Prime Minister, small C conservative. So his likes are rugby, the Labour Party, the trade unions, and like Harold Wilson, he loves the Boy Scouts. Did he wear tight trousers? I can't imagine him in tight trousers.
是的，我只是说，我只是在为“Woy”（罗伊·詹金斯的昵称）辩护。有时人们说卡拉汉，我认为并非不合理，他是最后一位担任首相的真正保守派，小写的“c”保守派。所以他的爱好是橄榄球、工党、工会，而且像哈罗德·威尔逊一样，他热爱童子军。他穿紧身裤吗？我无法想象他穿紧身裤的样子。

23
00:10:51.000 --> 00:11:32.000
Never. He wore long, baggy, high-wasted trousers. He hates it when his lefty activists criticise the police or the armed forces or the queen. The queen, he loves the queen. He's a teetotler. He hates any hint of permissiveness or sexual misbehavior. He and his wife Audrey met when they were 17 and 16 respectively, and they're incredibly devoted. When he was prime minister, he told his staff, there's a lovely bit in Sir Bernard Donahue carried on working for Callaghan after Wilson left, so we get these fantastic diaries. And Callahan says at one point, he's embarrassed by nudity on TV or on the stage. So whenever he sees nudity on TV, he has to turn it off, even though he's in his 60s.
绝不。他穿长长的、宽松的、高腰的裤子。他讨厌左翼活动家批评警察、军队或女王。女王，他爱女王。他是个禁酒主义者。他讨厌任何暗示宽容或性不检点的行为。他和他的妻子奥黛丽分别在17岁和16岁时相识，他们非常恩爱。当他担任首相时，他告诉他的员工，伯纳德·多纳休（Sir Bernard Donahue）爵士在威尔逊离开后继续为卡拉汉工作，所以我们得到了这些精彩的日记。卡拉汉在某个时候说，他对电视或舞台上的裸露感到尴尬。所以每当他在电视上看到裸露镜头时，他都必须关掉它，尽管他已经60多岁了。

24
00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:52.000
